Title: Human chorionic gonadotropin
Abstract: In 1905 Halbin hypothesized that the placenta elaborated a substance that was responsible for some of the 
maternal changes during pregnancy. 1 The ovarian hyperemia and luteotrophic effects of placental extracts were described
in 1913 by Aschner. 2 However, in 1927 Aschheim and Zondek firmly established the presence of human chorionic 
gonadotropin (choriogonadotropin, hCG) excreted in the urine of pregnant women as a hormone capable of luteinizing 
ovarian tissue. 3 Since then, much basic information has been learned about this trophoblastic hormone, sensitive 
techniques have been developed to detect and quantitate hCG in body fluids, and the clinical utility of measurements of 
hCG in the diagnosis and monitoring of pregnancy and trophoblastic tumors has been defined.Thieme Connect

